<section class="lesson-card">
<h2>11. A practical selection procedure</h2>
<ol class="steps"><li><strong>Define the experimental unit or interval. </strong>Is one observation a pipe, a 100 m length, an hour, or the space between events?</li><li><strong>Ask whether each unit has two outcomes. </strong>A fixed number of pass/fail or yes/no trials points to Binomial.</li><li><strong>Ask whether multiple events can occur in one interval. </strong>Random event counts inside time, length, area, or volume point to Poisson.</li><li><strong>Ask whether you are measuring the gap. </strong>Time or distance until the next event points to Exponential.</li><li><strong>Check assumptions. </strong>Binomial needs fixed n, constant p, and approximately independent trials. Poisson/Exponential need a reasonably constant rate and independent events.</li><li><strong>Select the operational tool. </strong>Binomial → p or np chart; Poisson → c or u chart; waiting time/distance → Exponential.</li></ol>
</section>
<section class="lesson-card">
<h2>12. When you see this, it means this</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>When you see this</th><th>It means this</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Large λ</td><td>Events are frequent; expected counts are higher and average gaps are shorter.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Small λ</td><td>Events are rare; expected counts are lower and average gaps are longer.</td></tr>
<tr><td>μ = λt</td><td>Expected count grows in direct proportion to the observation opportunity.</td></tr>
<tr><td>1/λ</td><td>The average waiting time or distance between consecutive events.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P(T&gt;t)</td><td>The probability of seeing zero events during the next t units.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Event rate changes over time</td><td>A simple stationary Poisson/Exponential model may be inappropriate or require stratification.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>
<section class="lesson-card">
<h2>13. Common mistakes and exam traps</h2>
<div class="grid-2">
<div class="trap-card"><strong>Assuming every count is Poisson</strong><p>A count of failed units out of a fixed sample is Binomial. A count of defects in an interval is Poisson.</p></div>
<div class="trap-card"><strong>Using Exponential for the number of events</strong><p>Exponential is continuous. Counts such as 0, 1, 2, … belong to Poisson.</p></div>
<div class="trap-card"><strong>Forgetting to align units</strong><p>A rate of 2 per 100 m must become 0.02 per metre when t is entered in metres.</p></div>
<div class="trap-card"><strong>Using a c chart with varying opportunity</strong><p>Larger units naturally have more defects. Use a u chart or an offset model when opportunity varies.</p></div>
<div class="trap-card"><strong>Assuming “memoryless” means equipment never ages</strong><p>It only applies when the constant-hazard Exponential model is reasonable. Wear-out failures often violate it.</p></div>
<div class="trap-card"><strong>Ignoring clustering</strong><p>If defects occur in bursts, the variance may exceed the mean. A basic Poisson model can underestimate variability.</p></div>
</div>
</section>
<!-- EDIT: Assumption checker -->
<section class="lesson-card" id="assumptions">
<h2>14. Assumption checker</h2>
<p class="section-intro">A formula can be mathematically correct and still be operationally misleading. Use this checklist as a screening tool before relying on a model.</p>
<div class="interactive-shell">
<div class="control"><label for="assumptionModel">Model to assess</label><select id="assumptionModel"><option value="binomial">Binomial</option><option value="poisson">Poisson</option><option value="exponential">Exponential</option></select></div>
<div class="assumption-grid" id="assumptionList"></div>
<div class="button-row"><button class="btn" id="assumptionAllBtn" type="button">Mark all supported</button><button class="btn secondary" id="assumptionResetBtn" type="button">Clear checklist</button></div>
<p aria-live="polite" class="status-line warn" id="assumptionResult"></p>
</div>
<table><thead><tr><th>Diagnostic clue</th><th>What it may indicate</th><th>Next action</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Poisson count variance far above the mean</td><td>Clustering, mixed rates, or omitted exposure effects</td><td>Stratify the data; consider negative-binomial or another overdispersed count model.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Poisson count variance far below the mean</td><td>Regular spacing, process constraints, or dependence</td><td>Review the event definition and whether occurrences inhibit one another.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Failure rate increases with age</td><td>Constant-hazard Exponential assumption is violated</td><td>Consider Weibull or a reliability model that allows changing hazard.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Binomial p changes by supplier, shift, or product</td><td>Trials do not share one constant probability</td><td>Stratify or model the probability using relevant predictors.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
</section>
<!-- EDIT: Software guidance -->
<section class="lesson-card" id="software">
<h2>15. Excel and Minitab guidance</h2>
<p>Software should execute the probability calculation; it should not choose the model for you. Define the random variable and confirm the assumptions first.</p>
<div class="sheet-grid">
<div class="software-card"><h3>Excel</h3>
<p><strong>Binomial:</strong></p><code class="code-line">=BINOM.DIST(x, n, p, FALSE)</code><p class="small">Exact probability P(X=x).</p>
<code class="code-line">=BINOM.DIST(x, n, p, TRUE)</code><p class="small">Cumulative probability P(X≤x).</p>
<p><strong>Poisson:</strong></p><code class="code-line">=POISSON.DIST(x, mean, FALSE)</code><p class="small">Exact probability, where mean = λt.</p>
<code class="code-line">=POISSON.DIST(x, mean, TRUE)</code><p class="small">Cumulative probability P(X≤x).</p>
<p><strong>Exponential:</strong></p><code class="code-line">=EXPON.DIST(t, lambda, TRUE)</code><p class="small">P(T≤t). For P(T&gt;t), use <code>1-EXPON.DIST(t,lambda,TRUE)</code>.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>Common Excel mistake:</strong> entering a rate per 100 m while t is measured in metres. Convert the units before multiplying λt.</div>
</div>
<div class="software-card"><h3>Minitab</h3>
<p><strong>Probability calculations:</strong> choose <em>Calc → Probability Distributions</em>, then select Binomial, Poisson, or Exponential.</p>
<ol><li>Select <strong>Probability</strong> for an exact discrete probability or density value.</li><li>Select <strong>Cumulative probability</strong> for P(X≤x) or P(T≤t).</li><li>Enter the correct parameter: n and p for Binomial; mean μ for Poisson; scale 1/λ for Exponential.</li><li>Store the result in a constant or column when documenting repeated calculations.</li></ol>
<p><strong>Attribute charts:</strong> choose <em>Stat → Control Charts → Attribute Charts</em>. Use <strong>C</strong> for constant opportunity and <strong>U</strong> when opportunity varies; supply subgroup sizes or opportunity values for the u chart.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>Common Minitab mistake:</strong> confusing Exponential scale with rate. The scale is the mean waiting time, 1/λ.</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="lesson-card" id="practice">
<h2>16. Practice activity</h2>
<p>A coating line inspects 30 panels. Each panel has a 5% chance of being rejected, while pinholes occur at an average rate of 4 per 200 m².</p>
<ol><li>Which distribution models the number of rejected panels?</li><li>What is the expected number of rejected panels?</li><li>Which distribution models the number of pinholes in the next 200 m²?</li><li>Which distribution models the inspected area until the next pinhole?</li><li>What is the mean area between pinholes?</li></ol>
<button aria-expanded="false" class="btn" id="revealPracticeBtn" type="button">Reveal answer</button>
<div class="reveal" id="practiceAnswer"><p><strong>1. Binomial.</strong> There are 30 fixed yes-or-no panel trials.</p><p><strong>2. Expected rejected panels = 1.5.</strong> np = 30 × 0.05 = 1.5.</p><p><strong>3. Poisson.</strong> The response is the number of pinhole events in a fixed area.</p><p><strong>4. Exponential.</strong> The response is the continuous area until the next pinhole.</p><p><strong>5. Mean gap = 50 m².</strong> Rate = 4/200 = 0.02 per m², so 1/λ = 50 m².</p></div>
<div class="extension-practice">
<h3>Extension: choose a chart and test a waiting-time claim</h3>
<p>Eight inspected coil lengths are 100, 150, 80, 200, 120, 100, 250, and 100 m. Their defect counts are 2, 5, 1, 7, 3, 2, 9, and 2. The long-run rate is estimated at 0.03 defect per metre.</p>
<ol><li>Should the team use a c chart or u chart?</li><li>What is the probability of travelling more than 50 m before the next defect under the Exponential model?</li><li>State one assumption that must be checked.</li></ol>
<button aria-expanded="false" class="btn secondary" id="revealExtensionBtn" type="button">Reveal extension answer</button>
<div class="reveal" id="extensionAnswer"><p><strong>1. Use a u chart.</strong> Inspected lengths vary, so raw counts have unequal opportunities. Plot defects per metre (or per 100 m) with limits that reflect each length.</p><p><strong>2. P(T&gt;50)=e<sup>−0.03×50</sup>=e<sup>−1.5</sup>≈0.2231.</strong> There is about a 22.31% chance of more than 50 m without the next defect.</p><p><strong>3. Check that the defect rate is reasonably constant and events are not clustered.</strong> A bursty process can invalidate the basic Poisson–Exponential assumptions.</p></div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="lesson-card" id="quiz">
<h2>17. Mini quiz</h2>
<form id="quizForm"><div class="quiz-question" data-answer="b"><p><strong>1. Which question is Binomial?</strong></p><label class="option"><input name="q1" type="radio" value="a"/> How many scratches occur in 100 m?</label><label class="option"><input name="q1" type="radio" value="b"/> How many of 20 pipes fail?</label><label class="option"><input name="q1" type="radio" value="c"/> How many metres until the next scratch?</label><div class="feedback">Binomial models the number of selected outcomes in a fixed number of yes-or-no trials.</div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-answer="c"><p><strong>2. Which question is Poisson?</strong></p><label class="option"><input name="q2" type="radio" value="a"/> How many of 50 welds fail?</label><label class="option"><input name="q2" type="radio" value="b"/> How long until the next failure?</label><label class="option"><input name="q2" type="radio" value="c"/> How many inclusions occur in one plate?</label><div class="feedback">Poisson models random event counts in a fixed interval or opportunity.</div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-answer="a"><p><strong>3. Which question is Exponential?</strong></p><label class="option"><input name="q3" type="radio" value="a"/> How many hours until the next breakdown?</label><label class="option"><input name="q3" type="radio" value="b"/> How many breakdowns occur this week?</label><label class="option"><input name="q3" type="radio" value="c"/> How many of 10 machines fail inspection?</label><div class="feedback">Exponential models continuous waiting time or distance to the next event.</div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-answer="b"><p><strong>4. What is the key difference between Binomial and Poisson counts?</strong></p><label class="option"><input name="q4" type="radio" value="a"/> Binomial is continuous and Poisson is discrete.</label><label class="option"><input name="q4" type="radio" value="b"/> Binomial has a fixed number of two-outcome trials; Poisson counts events in an interval.</label><label class="option"><input name="q4" type="radio" value="c"/> There is no difference.</label><div class="feedback">Both are discrete counts, but they are generated by different mechanisms.</div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-answer="c"><p><strong>5. Which control-chart pairing is correct?</strong></p><label class="option"><input name="q5" type="radio" value="a"/> p and np are Poisson; c and u are Binomial.</label><label class="option"><input name="q5" type="radio" value="b"/> All four are Exponential.</label><label class="option"><input name="q5" type="radio" value="c"/> p and np are Binomial; c and u are Poisson.</label><div class="feedback">p and np charts are based on Binomial data, while c and u charts are based on Poisson counts.</div></div><div class="button-row">
<button class="btn" type="submit">Submit quiz</button>
<button class="btn secondary" id="resetQuizBtn" type="button">Reset quiz</button>
</div><div aria-live="polite" id="quizScore"></div></form>
</section>
<section class="lesson-card" id="summary">
<h2>18. Final summary</h2>
<ul><li>Binomial models the number of selected outcomes in a fixed number of yes-or-no trials.</li><li>Poisson models the number of random events in a fixed interval or opportunity.</li><li>Exponential models the waiting time or distance until the next Poisson event.</li><li>Binomial counts are bounded by n; Poisson counts are not fixed by a trial count.</li><li>For Binomial data, the expected count is np.</li><li>For Poisson data, the expected count is μ = λt.</li><li>For Exponential data, the mean gap is 1/λ.</li><li>p and np charts are Binomial; c and u charts are Poisson.</li></ul>
<div class="big-memory">Decision rule: fixed yes-or-no trials → Binomial; event count in an interval → Poisson; waiting time or distance → Exponential.</div>
<p><strong>Remember this:</strong> <em>Count selected trials, count random events, or measure the spaces.</em></p>
<p><strong>Suggested next lesson:</strong> Attribute control charts—choosing among p, np, c, and u charts.</p>
</section>
<section class="lesson-card" id="references">
<h2>References</h2>
<p class="small">Montgomery, D. C. <em>Introduction to Statistical Quality Control</em>. Wiley. Ross, S. M. <em>Introduction to Probability Models</em>. Academic Press.</p>
</section>
